ide we currently have a  use reduction prohibitionist approach so  I want to talk about some of the terms  which is very important that people  understand the terms were using  criminalization prohibition is the  general way in which drugs which are  illegal are controlled around the world  and basically possession or use of a  drug in some countries in the UK  thankfully is a crime that can be  punished by criminal sanctions including  the death penalty in some countries deep  penalization or decriminalization is  what I'm gonna argue is the right way  forward and this means no criminal  sanctions you can apply civil sanctions  for instance if you catch someone with a  a drug which is decriminalize you can  find them you can withhold their driving  license you can often as they do in  Portugal use what they call dissuasion  processes to help people understand the  harms of using those drugs and but you  don't get a criminal sanction so you  don't drive the criminal underworld  legalization generally is taken to mean  anything from a fully open market where  you can sell any drug to anyone just  rare in the world but I suppose in some  parts of the favelas in South America  most things are available if you want or  it could mean a regulated market like we  have currently with alcohol and tobacco  in Britain where there are age limits  and safety limits so there are those  terms in mind as we go through my talk  so I'm gonna argue the decriminalization  is the right approach for a number of  reasons the first is it avoids the  massive damages that criminalization  produces the second is a  decriminalization is actually  proportionate to the health harms of  drugs  the third is it that it will greatly  facilitate research and clinical  innovation and the fourth is it works we  know from over 40 years of  decriminalization of cannabis and nearly  18 years of decriminalizing or drugs in  Portugal that this approach works so  let's look at the current policy that we  have in Britain well it's a punishment  based policy and the first is it's  biased about the drugs which we control  it's dishonest and therefore immoral we  don't control tobacco we don't control  alcohol but we control other drugs which  I'm gonna argue are actually less  harmful there so this makes it a  peculiarly dishonest and ineffective  policy one of the reasons we have this  peculiarity of almost much you might  call sort of scientific nihilism about  drug policies is there is no definition  either in the UN conventions or in the  UK Drugs Act about what a drug is or who  should say what a drug is and basically  that means that it's left to politicians  to newspapers and the drinks industry to  define what a drug is and of course they  do it not based on harms or risks but  based on political or economic and  benefits so here's a wonderful example  of how the drinks industry alters  people's perceptions as what a drug is  say no to drugs that way you'll have  more time to drink now of course  everyone knows that alcohols a drug but  it isn't controlled as a drug under the  misuse of Drugs Act why not  because historically it's been exempt  why because people like to drink and the  drinks industry has thanks very best to  make sure that people don't understand  the risks of drink and they often  distort the harms of other drugs and try  to minimize the harms of alcohol so my  definition of a drug is here something a  politician once years but now regrets  like a couple of examples in my career  of Jackie Smith a Home Secretary I  locked swords win over the comparative  harms of ecstasy versus horse riding she  said I smoked cannabis  but I didn't enjoy and you think well  why would you bother but maybe you had  to do that to get into the labour party  in Oxford when she was there David  Cameron I did things when young I  shouldn't have we all did and great  thing about David he tended to do drugs  which began with the same letter as his  name see drugs like cannabis and cocaine  so we know there's a strong tradition of  politicians using drugs we've had this  recent example for instance of Michael  Gove having been quite into cocaine  Parks's but he now regrets that and but  he's very glad he wasn't caught and  wasn't imprisoned for it when we're  looking at drugs and drug policy what  are we trying to do well we are trying  most of us will argue to reduce harms  and perhaps the most important  significant harm of a drug is is death  now the latest data we have on the  earths from different drugs on the left  hand side you see tobacco over 80,000  premature deaths alcohol about 28,000  opiates reached all-time high last year  of nearly 18,000 at 1,800 sorry  and then cocaine paracetamol and feta  mean ecstasy so the big killers are  tobacco some economists say that tobacco  deaths are a matter their core what they  call good deaths because people who  smoke pay a lot of tax during their  lifetime and then they die before they  can draw their pensions so actually they  their net contributors to the economy  whereas alcohol deaths are a must and  more spread out across the age range and  they do have a very powerful impact on  young people and their opiates for the  third so these are the three big killers  and you might have think that those  would be the drugs we wanted to focus  most of our attention on but in fact  largely the hysteria that you read about  drugs in the newspaper in the media is  focused on drugs there on the right hand  side ecstasy in cannabis etc which have  very low levels of harm  and one of the most remarkable studies I  was involved with was this one which was  published about ten years ago now in The  Lancet and it was the most systematic  analysis of drug harms it had never been  done we determined that there were 16  different arms of drugs and we took  these 20 drugs and scaled them on these  16 homes nine of the harms are the harm  to the user and seven of the harms are  harms to society and 